1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,671
A contract is a theorem about a
definition.

2
00:00:02,671 --> 00:00:05,094
Here is the smallest one worth writing.

3
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:11,562
Here is a very small program. maxOf
returns the larger of two natural numbers.

4
00:00:11,562 --> 00:00:14,756
It is correct, but nothing about it says
so.

5
00:00:15,667 --> 00:00:19,583
The specification is another term in the
same language.

6
00:00:19,583 --> 00:00:22,432
For all a and b, a is at most maxOf a b.

7
00:00:22,432 --> 00:00:26,847
That is a statement about every pair of
numbers, not a sample.

8
00:00:27,867 --> 00:00:31,373
The proof is a program of that statement's
type.

9
00:00:31,373 --> 00:00:38,751
We unfold the definition, split on the
comparison, and in each branch the
arithmetic closes the goal.

10
00:00:38,751 --> 00:00:47,589
Lean prints the goal state after every
step, so the proof is not a story about
the code; it is the machine's own working.

11
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:51,781
Two kinds of error now become visible.

12
00:00:51,781 --> 00:00:59,287
An error of reasoning, connecting things
wrongly, is rejected: the kernel will not
accept the step.

13
00:00:59,287 --> 00:01:05,732
An error of interpretation,
misunderstanding what the code means,
leaves an artefact.

14
00:01:05,732 --> 00:01:10,660
The specification you wrote is there to be
read, and iterated on.

15
00:01:11,767 --> 00:01:14,405
The proof is checked, not reviewed.

16
00:01:14,405 --> 00:01:16,364
Next, two ways to specify.

